  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nCurrently, a deep upper level trough is situated over the \nnortheastern US and eastern Canada while the ridge axis extends over \nthe west coast of the US placing the Central Plains under weak \nnorthwesterly flow. Anomalous heights over the region will lead to \nafternoon temperatures reaching above normal through early next \nweek. High temperatures across the state will reach into the middle \nto upper 80s each afternoon. A few locations in southern Kansas \ncould top out in the lower 90s. By Friday, a trough will begin \ndigging into the Pac NW and a corresponding surface low will develop \nover the Northern Rockies and southern Canadian Prairies. The \nresulting tightening pressure gradient over the Plains will lead to \nbreezy southwesterly winds for Fri-Mon. Sustained winds between 15-\n25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph are possible each afternoon.\n\nThe next chance for a pattern change will then come next week as a \ncutoff low develops over southern California on Monday and lifts \ntoward the Plains by mid week. While confidence in exact timing and \nimpacts remains low, continuity between model guidance suggests \nincreased rain chances and cooler temperatures for the tail end of \nthe forecast time frame and beyond.",
